   "fen": "rnb1kb1r/pp1ppppp/1q3n2/2p5/3P1B2/2P5/PP2PPPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Qb6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Because after the queen to b6, White is borderline worse — b2 is hit and it is hard to find a move. Defend it with the queen and d4 comes loose. Develop the knight to c6, White plays e3, and Black plays d6 or d5. And here is a standard London tactic with the queen on b6: the bishop to f5, forking the queen, rook and knight — White is busted. Deeper still, the knight to d3 uses the pin to drag the king out, then the knight to f4, and Black is clearly better."
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 2565.5,
   "fen": "rnb1kb1r/pp1ppppp/1q3n2/2p5/3P1B2/2P5/PP1NPPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 2 4",
   "line": [
    "Nd2"
   ],
   "spoken": "White's better try, which few know, is the knight to d2 — taking on b2 leads to insane complications."
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 2574,
   "fen": "rnb1kb1r/pp2pppp/1q3n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P5/PP1NPPPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Simplest is just d5, reaching a superior version of a standard London."
